NAME
    asm - the on-target two-pass assembler

SYNOPSIS
    asm src.asm out.bin

DESCRIPTION
    Assembles a P8X assembly source file into a binary, entirely on the
    machine. Pair it with edit (or vi) for a self-hosted edit/assemble/run
    loop. asm is a /bin program.

    A `;#use NAME` line (at the start of a line) appends the shared include
    /lib/NAME.inc after the program, so a command can share helpers the way
    the C compiler's //#use shares /lib/lib_NAME.c. Up to 4 per file.

    A `.include "path"` line appends that file after the program, with the path
    resolved relative to the including source's own directory (so
    `.include "../generators/memmap.inc"` finds it a directory over). Equates are
    resolved in a first pass, so the include may sit anywhere and still define
    symbols the body uses. One .include per file. This is how /src/os-bios builds
    the OS and monitor on the machine.

    Both the SRC and OUT arguments are path-aware -- a full path, not just a
    12-char root-only name -- so you can assemble a source out of a /src tree
    straight into a build-output directory. This is what the /src Makefiles rely on. A
    relative name (no leading /) resolves against the current directory, like the
    shell -- so `asm T.ASM out.bin` reads a `cc ... >T.ASM` redirect from any CWD.

    If the output file already exists it is overwritten (the old copy is removed
    first, then rewritten), so re-assembling never leaves a duplicate. The old
    data sectors are reclaimed by the next `pack`.

EXAMPLES
    asm hello.asm hello.bin
    run hello.bin
    asm /src/commands/asm/cat.asm cat.bin   (cat.asm does `;#use stdin`)
    asm /src/commands/asm/pwd.asm /src/commands/asm/bin/pwd.bin

SEE ALSO
    edit, vi, run, cc, sh
